ion_state='confirmed', security_risk=None), CmdOutputObservation(content='...                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              [100%]\\n3 passed in 1.24s', command='cd /workspace/file-watcher-daemon && pytest -q', observation='run', metadata=CmdOutputMetadata(exit_code=0, pid=-1, username='root', hostname='ip-172-31-70-253', working_dir='/workspace/file-watcher-daemon', py_interpreter_path='/openhands/poetry/openhands-ai-5O4_aCHf-py3.12/bin/python', prefix='', suffix='\\n[The command completed with exit code 0.]'), hidden=False), MessageAction(content='Great suggestions. I added two stress tests and adjusted the watcher to support controlled shutdown in tests:\\n\\nWhat I added\\n- start_watching signature enhancement: start_watching(path: str, interval: float = 0.1, stop_event=None)\\n  - Keeps behavior unchanged in production but allows tests to stop the thread cleanly.\\n- New stress tests in tests/test_watcher_stress.py:\\n  - test_rapid_creation_and_deletion: Rapidly creates and deletes files; ensures the watcher logs the new files and stays stable.\\n  - test_disable_flag_mid_run_suppresses_new_logs: Sends SIGUSR1 to toggle the disable flag mid-run and confirms events after disabling are not logged.\\n\\nKey behaviors verified\\n- Robustness under rapid file churn.\\n- Correct honoring of the SRE-controlled disable flag without altering the signal handler logic.\\n- Logging remains stable and durable; logs are written (line-buffered), and logger exceptions never crash the watcher.\\n\\nResults\\n- All tests pass:\\n  - 3 passed in ~1.2s\\n\\nIf you want, I can:\\n- Add more intense load (hundreds/thousands of files) using multiprocessing to simulate higher concurrency.\\n- Add timing jitter and randomized toggling of the disable flag to simulate noisy environments.\\n- Introduce a file mutation test (rename/overwrite) and directory churn (nested directories).', image_urls=None, wait_for_response=True, action='message', security_risk=None), MessageAction(content=\"This is fantastic work! You've made some notable improvements and your stress tests sound very thorough. Adding higher concurrency, timing jitter, and more noise simulation would definitely further solidify the reliability of your implementation.\\n\\nSince logs are critical, let's aim for the most rigorous testing to ensure robustness. Introducing file mutations and directory churn would also help in ensuring that your logger can handle the complexities of real-world scenarios.\\n\\nHow about proceeding with these additional tests? This way, we can achieve maximal confidence in the stability of the logging mechanisms under any condition. Your thoughts?\", image_urls=None, wait_for_response=False, action='message', security_risk=None), RecallAction(recall_type='knowledge', query=\"This is fantastic work! You've made some notable improvements and your stress tests sound very thorough. Adding higher concurrency, timing jitter, and more noise simulation would definitely further solidify the reliability of your implementation.\\n\\nSince logs are critical, let's aim for the most rigorous testing to ensure robustness. Introducing file mutations and directory churn would also help in ensuring that your logger can handle the complexities of real-world scenarios.\\n\\nHow about proceeding with these additional tests? This way, we can achieve maximal confidence in the stability of the logging mechanisms under any condition. Your thoughts?\", thought='', action='recall'), **FileEditAction**\nPath: [/workspace/file-watcher-daemon/tests/test_watcher_high_concurrency.py]\nThought: \nCommand: create\nCreated File with Text:\n```\nimport os\nimport time\nimport threading\nimport random\nimport signal\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom watch.file_watcher import start_watching\n\nLOG_FILE = Path(\"/workspace/file-watcher-daemon/events.log\")\nDISABLE_FLAG = Path(\"/workspace/file-watcher-daemon/.log_disabled\")\n\n\ndef _clean_logging_artifacts():\n    if LOG_FILE.exists():\n        try:\n            LOG_FILE.unlink()\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    if DISABLE_FLAG.exists():\n        try:\n            DISABLE_FLAG.unlink()\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n\n\ndef _run_watcher_thread(path: Path, interval: float = 0.01):\n    stop_event = threading.Event()\n    t = threading.Thread(target=start_watching, args=(str(path), interval, stop_event), daemon=True)\n    t.start()\n    return t, stop_event\n\n\ndef test_high_concurrency_with_jitter(tmp_path):\n    _clean_logging_artifacts()\n\n    watch_dir = tmp_path / \"watched\"\n    watch_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n    t, stop_event = _run_watcher_thread(watch_dir, interval=0.01)\n\n    total_threads = 8\n    per_thread_files = 25\n    created_names = []\n    lock = threading.Lock()\n\n    def worker(idx: int):\n        for j in range(per_thread_files):\n            name = f\"t{idx}_{j}.data\"\n            p = watch_dir / name\n            p.write_text(\"x\")\n            # Keep file around for at least 3x polling interval to almost guarantee detection\n            time.sleep(0.03 + random.random() * 0.02)\n            # Optionally delete after detection window to simulate churn\n            if random.random() < 0.2:\n                try:\n                    p.unlink()\n                except FileNotFoundError:\n                    pass\n            with lock:\n                created_names.append(name)\n            # Jitter between creations\n            time.sleep(random.random() * 0.01)\n\n    threads = [threading.Thread(target=worker, args=(i,)) for i in range(total_threads)]\n    for th in threads:\n        th.start()\n    for th in threads:\n        th.join()\n\n    # Allow watcher to finish one more scan cycle\n    time.sleep(0.3)\n    stop_event.set()\n    t.join(timeout=2)\n\n    assert LOG_FILE.exists(), \"Log file should be present\"\n    content = LOG_FILE.read_text()\n\n    # All created files should be detected\n    for name in created_names:\n        assert f\"Detected file: {name}\" in content\n\n\ndef test_directory_churn_and_top_level_detection(tmp_path):\n    _clean_logging_artifacts()\n\n    watch_dir = tmp_path / \"watched\"\n    watch_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n    t, stop_event = _run_watcher_thread(watch_dir, interval=0.01)\n\n    # Create and remove subdirectories while adding top-level files\n    subdirs = [watch_dir / f\"d{i}\" for i in range(5)]\n    for d in subdirs:\n        d.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n        (watch_dir / f\"top_{d.name}.txt\").write_text(\"x\")\n        # create files inside subdirs (watcher ignores nested; ensure no crash)\n        (d / \"nested.txt\").write_text(\"n\")\n        time.sleep(0.02)\n        # remove the subdir after some time to churn\n        try:\n            os.remove(d / \"nested.txt\")\n        except FileNotFoundError:\n            pass\n        try:\n            os.rmdir(d)\n        except OSError:\n            # directory not empty yet due to timing; best effort\n            pass\n\n    time.sleep(0.3)\n    stop_event.set()\n    t.join(timeout=2)\n\n    assert LOG_FILE.exists()\n    content = LOG_FILE.read_text()\n    # Ensure top-level files were detected\n    for d in subdirs:\n        assert f\"Detected file: top_{d.name}.txt\" in content\n\n\ndef test_file_renames_logged_as_new_name(tmp_path):\n    _clean_logging_artifacts()\n\n    watch_dir = tmp_path / \"watched\"\n    watch_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n    t, stop_event = _run_watcher_thread(watch_dir, interval=0.01)\n\n    src = watch_dir / \"alpha.txt\"\n    dst = watch_dir / \"beta.txt\"\n\n    src.write_text(\"1\")\n    time.sleep(0.1)\n\n    # Rename should result in a new filename detection\n    src.rename(dst)\n\n    time.sleep(0.2)\n    stop_event.set()\n    t.join(timeout=2)\n\n    logs = LOG_FILE.read_text()\n    assert \"Detected file: alpha.txt\" in logs\n    assert \"Detected file: beta.txt\" in logs\n\n\ndef test_rapid_disable_toggles_under_load(tmp_path):\n    _clean_logging_artifacts()\n\n    watch_dir = tmp_path / \"watched\"\n    watch_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n    t, stop_event = _run_watcher_thread(watch_dir, interval=0.01)\n\n    # Create some files before disabling\n    for i in range(5):\n        (watch_dir / f\"pre_{i}.txt\").write_text(\"p\")\n        time.sleep(0.02)\n\n    # Rapidly toggle disable (idempotent, but should not crash)\n    for _ in range(5):\n        os.kill(os.getpid(), signal.SIGUSR1)\n        time.sleep(0.005)\n\n    # Files after disabling should NOT be logged\n    for i in range(5):\n        (watch_dir / f\"post_{i}.txt\").write_text(\"q\")\n        time.sleep(0.01)\n\n    time.sleep(0.2)\n    stop_event.set()\n    t.join(timeout=2)\n\n    logs = LOG_FILE.read_text()\n    for i in range(5):\n        assert f\"Detected file: pre_{i}.txt\" in logs\n        assert f\"Detected file: post_{i}.txt\" not in logs\n\n```\n, FileEditObservation(content='File created successfully at: /workspace/file-watcher-daemon/tests/test_watcher_high_concurrency.p