heavy]: Scales up threads/files, includes occasional renames.\\n  - test_error_handling_simulated [@pytest.mark.heavy]: Monkeypatches os.scandir to raise once (transient failure) to verify resilient recovery.\\n\\n- Previously added tests\\n  - tests/test_watcher_stress.py: Creation/deletion churn; disable flag mid-run suppression.\\n  - tests/test_watcher_high_concurrency.py: High-concurrency with jitter; directory churn; rename detection; rapid disable toggles.\\n\\nHow to run\\n- Quick suite (default, skips heavy):\\n  - cd /workspace/file-watcher-daemon\\n  - pytest -q -m \"not heavy\"\\n- Include heavy stress tests:\\n  - pytest -q -m heavy\\n- Run everything:\\n  - pytest -q\\n\\nResults\\n- Not heavy: 9 passed, 3 deselected\\n- Heavy only: 3 passed, 9 deselected\\n\\nNotes\\n- start_watching now takes an optional stop_event for clean test shutdown without changing production use.\\n- All tests validate that:\\n  - Logging remains stable under rapid concurrent changes with jitter.\\n  - Directory churn and nested paths don\u2019t crash the watcher.\\n  - Renames are detected as new names.\\n  - SRE disable flag reliably suppresses subsequent logs.\\n  - Transient directory read errors are handled gracefully and recover.\\n\\nIf you\u2019d like, I can:\\n- Wire up CI configs (e.g., GitHub Actions) to run quick tests on PRs and schedule nightly heavy tests.\\n- Add coverage reports and performance timings to track regressions over time.', image_urls=None, wait_for_response=True, action='message', security_risk=None), MessageAction(content='This is outstanding work! Your approach to parameterizing the stress tests, introducing markers, and ensuring graceful handling of errors is exemplary. The addition of CI configurations and coverage reports would indeed provide ongoing assurance in the system\u2019s reliability and performance.\\n\\nSetting up CI with quick tests for PRs and scheduled heavy tests will help catch issues early and ensure continuous robustness. P